/-!
# VIII04 — true ε-linearisation of the frozen radial residuals on the RN background

Trial family (odd parity, equal rotation): `J_ε = ε j`, `a_ε = ε b`, `w_ε = ε v`, and the background
`f = f0`, `m = m0`, `N = N0`, `p = p0` kept fixed. No differentiability is needed for the *exact*
identities below (`deriv (ε·b) = ε·deriv b` holds for every function, `deriv_const_mul_field'`):

* `F_eps`, `M_eps`, `C_eps`: Eqs. 24a/24b/25 are `background + ε²·(explicit)` — no `ε¹` term;
* `maxwell_eps`: Eq. 24c is exactly `ε ·` (Eq. 24c at `(b, j)`);
* `q_eps`, `ell_eps`: `q = (N/f)(p' + ε² v b')`, `ℓ = ε K v' - 4 q ε b` (the `w a'` coupling is `O(ε²)`;
  `p` has no first-order correction in this odd-parity family — a choice of the trial family).

On RN (`maxwell_RN`): Eq. 24c at `(b, j)` is `m0 · (L b - 2Qj/R³)` with
`L b = (P b')' - V b`, `P = N0/R`, `V = 1/R + 4Q²/R³`; the normalising factor is `m0 = N0/R`.
`LinSys` is the first-order system, defined by the **true ε-derivatives at ε = 0** of the four frozen
residuals and of the frozen first integrals (`δq = 0`, `δℓ = -2j`); `linSys_iff` proves it is
equivalent to `L b = 2Qj/R³` and `v' = -2(j + 2Qb)/R³`. `Lop_expand` gives `L b` in terms of genuine
first/second derivatives for `b ∈ C²`. Nothing here produces a nonzero-ε PaperBVP solution.

Four of the six `LinSys` clauses (24a, 24b, 25 and `δq = 0`) hold automatically for every `b, v` on `I`
(`eps_derivs`); the content is 24c and `δℓ = -2j`, as expected for the odd sector. Setting the even
perturbations `δf = δm = δN = δp = δQ = 0` is a choice of the trial family; first-order even/odd decoupling
is not formalised here.
-/

